  "worked_solution": "First, we write $1\\,\\frac{3}{7}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{3}{7}=\\frac{(1\\times 7)+3}{7}=\\frac{10}{7}\n$$\n\nWe also write  $4\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n4\\,\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{(4\\times 2)+1}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10}{7}\\times \\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this product by swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10}{7}\\times \\frac{9}{2} & =\\frac{10}{2}\\times \\frac{9}{7} \\\\  & =5\\times \\frac{9}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{45}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{45}{7}=6\\,\\text{R}3=6\\,\\frac{3}{7}\n$$",
